1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:03,655
July 14, 1882.

2
00:00:03,695 --> 00:00:08,277
A teamster hauling wood up a mountain road
in southeastern Arizona

3
00:00:08,317 --> 00:00:12,940
notices a man sitting against an oak tree,
right beside the creek.

4
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:17,395
From a distance, he looks like he's
resting.

5
00:00:17,435 --> 00:00:21,650
Two women had ridden past him the day
before and thought exactly that.

6
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,480
He wasn't resting.

7
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:31,098
The man was Johnny Ringo — one of the most
feared gunmen in the territory,

8
00:00:31,138 --> 00:00:35,435
an outlaw tied to the same feud that
exploded at the O.K. Corral.

9
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:39,970
He had a revolver in his right hand and a
single bullet wound in his head.

10
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,650
His boots were gone.

11
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:45,425
His feet were wrapped in strips torn from
his own undershirt.

12
00:00:45,465 --> 00:00:48,665
His gun belt was buckled on upside down.

13
00:00:48,705 --> 00:00:50,970
And part of his scalp had been cut away.

14
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:55,055
The local jury called it suicide.

15
00:00:55,095 --> 00:00:57,869
Almost nobody who has studied the case
since

16
00:00:57,909 --> 00:01:00,340
has been comfortable with that answer.

17
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,595
Those mountains hold more than one
unsolved story.

18
00:01:05,635 --> 00:01:08,910
They were the homeland of the Chiricahua
Apache.

19
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:13,492
They sheltered outlaws, gave their name to
a warrior who escaped

20
00:01:13,532 --> 00:01:17,430
a prison train, and turned a blind woman
into a legend.

21
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:20,560
They were made by a volcano so violent it
makes

22
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,330
Mount St. Helens look like a firecracker.

23
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:30,169
And on October 20, 2026, they became
America's newest national

24
00:01:30,209 --> 00:01:35,100
park — the 64th, and the first new one in
nearly six years.

25
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:39,060
This is Chiricahua.

26
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:54,260
For centuries before any of this was a
park, these mountains were home to the

27
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:58,934
Chiricahua Apache — four related bands
whose territory stretched across

28
00:01:58,974 --> 00:02:03,280
southeastern Arizona, southwestern New
Mexico and northern Mexico.

29
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:06,515
They called themselves Ndé — "the people."

30
00:02:06,555 --> 00:02:10,684
The National Park Service describes them
as superb warriors who

31
00:02:10,724 --> 00:02:14,760
fiercely resisted colonization for more
than three centuries.

32
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:21,008
For more than a decade, the government had
tried to force the

33
00:02:21,048 --> 00:02:26,155
Chiricahua Apache onto the San Carlos
reservation, far to the north.

34
00:02:26,195 --> 00:02:29,976
Many refused. Small bands broke away again
and again — the most

35
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:33,510
famous led by a Bedonkohe warrior named
Geronimo.

36
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:39,485
By the summer of 1886, Geronimo's free
band was down to 37

37
00:02:39,525 --> 00:02:43,855
people: eighteen warriors, thirteen women
and six children —

38
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:47,715
chased by thousands of soldiers across two
countries.

39
00:02:47,755 --> 00:02:51,066
In early September 1886, in Skeleton
Canyon, just

40
00:02:51,106 --> 00:02:54,895
southeast of the Chiricahua Mountains,
they surrendered.

41
00:02:54,935 --> 00:02:58,545
Months earlier, at another surrender, he
had told an

42
00:02:58,585 --> 00:03:01,915
Army general: "Once I moved about like the
wind.

43
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:04,590
Now I surrender to you, and that is all."

44
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:09,815
What happened next is one of the darkest
chapters of the era.

45
00:03:09,855 --> 00:03:13,715
The Army didn't just send Geronimo's band
into exile.

46
00:03:13,755 --> 00:03:18,106
It sent nearly every Chiricahua Apache —
including the scouts

47
00:03:18,146 --> 00:03:22,065
who had served the Army and helped track
Geronimo down.

48
00:03:22,105 --> 00:03:27,080
They were loaded onto trains as prisoners
of war and sent to Florida.

49
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:31,665
They would not be free again for 27 years.

50
00:03:31,705 --> 00:03:36,935
They were moved from Florida to Alabama,
and then to Fort Sill in Oklahoma.

51
00:03:36,975 --> 00:03:42,500
In the damp prison camps, disease killed
many of them, children most of all.

52
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:47,600
Geronimo himself died at Fort Sill in
1909, still a prisoner.

53
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:53,403
And here's a connection most visitors
never hear: during the

54
00:03:53,443 --> 00:03:57,167
Geronimo campaign, Buffalo Soldiers of the
Tenth Cavalry

55
00:03:57,207 --> 00:04:00,730
camped inside what is now the park, in
Bonita Canyon.

56
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:05,280
Some of those soldiers later helped guard
the prisoners on the train east.

57
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:09,153
Before they left, they carved their names
into stone and

58
00:04:09,193 --> 00:04:12,680
built a small monument to President James
Garfield.

59
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,320
Archaeologists are still studying the
site.

60
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:22,675
I'm dressed in the uniform of the United
States

61
00:04:22,715 --> 00:04:28,330
Army cavalry of the 1870s and the 1880s.

62
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:32,092
Somewhere in Missouri, as one of those
trains

63
00:04:32,132 --> 00:04:35,520
labored up a steep grade, a man named
Massai jumped.

64
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:39,500
Massai had been an Army scout.

65
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:41,535
He was on the train anyway.

66
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:45,861
According to the story his family later
told, he became convinced

67
00:04:45,901 --> 00:04:49,855
the prisoners were going to be killed —
and he chose to run.

68
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:54,059
Traveling at night, he made his way back
across hundreds of miles

69
00:04:54,099 --> 00:04:57,720
of unfamiliar country to the mountains of
the Southwest.

70
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:03,540
What we know about his life after that
comes largely from his own family.

71
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:07,823
He took a young Mescalero woman,
Zanagoliche, from her people and

72
00:05:07,863 --> 00:05:11,908
made her his wife, and for many years they
lived hidden in the

73
00:05:11,948 --> 00:05:15,400
mountains, raising children far from any
reservation.

74
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:18,950
Settlers blamed him for raids and thefts —
some of

75
00:05:18,990 --> 00:05:22,540
which, historians now suspect, he never
committed.

76
00:05:24,370 --> 00:05:29,300
Around 1906, Massai was killed by a posse
in New Mexico.

77
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:32,644
His daughter, Alberta Begay, later shared
the

78
00:05:32,684 --> 00:05:35,765
family's memories with historian Eve Ball.

79
00:05:35,805 --> 00:05:38,757
Her mother always insisted he was no
murderer —

80
00:05:38,797 --> 00:05:41,470
that he fought only to protect his family.

81
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:50,155
Today the highest viewpoint you can drive
to in the park is called Massai Point.

82
00:05:50,195 --> 00:05:53,752
Even the Park Service admits the name may
rest on a

83
00:05:53,792 --> 00:05:56,785
mistaken settler story about a horse
thief.

84
00:05:56,825 --> 00:06:01,740
The family that ran the nearby ranch asked
that it be named for him anyway.

85
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:08,200
Stand there at sunset and you can see why
anyone would fight to come home.

86
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:13,600
The canyons drop away in every direction,
and the valleys below turn gold.

87
00:06:15,410 --> 00:06:19,460
But the story of how the Chiricahua lost
these mountains began

88
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:24,250
twenty-five years earlier — with a
misunderstanding inside an Army tent.

89
00:06:30,110 --> 00:06:34,576
In the mid-1800s, the most powerful leader
among the Chiricahua

90
00:06:34,616 --> 00:06:38,796
was a man named Cochise, of the Chokonen
band, whose people

91
00:06:38,836 --> 00:06:42,770
lived around Apache Pass and the mountains
to the west.

92
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:49,300
A quick note before we go on: there is no
known photograph of Cochise.

93
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:53,210
Images online labeled with his name show
other people.

94
00:06:53,250 --> 00:06:58,150
So in this story, you'll see the land he
knew — not a face we can't verify.

95
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:05,572
In January of 1861, raiders struck a ranch
south of Fort Buchanan, drove

96
00:07:05,612 --> 00:07:10,675
off cattle, and kidnapped a twelve-year-
old boy named Felix Ward.

97
00:07:10,715 --> 00:07:14,315
The Army assumed Cochise was responsible.

98
00:07:14,355 --> 00:07:18,340
The Park Service's own history of
Chiricahua says the raid was

99
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:21,820
likely the work of a different Apache
group entirely.

100
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:27,664
A young lieutenant named George Bascom
rode to Apache

101
00:07:27,704 --> 00:07:31,480
Pass with 54 soldiers and invited Cochise
to talk.

102
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:37,028
On February 4, inside an Army tent, Bascom
accused him of the kidnapping and

103
00:07:37,068 --> 00:07:41,740
announced that Cochise would be held until
the boy was returned.

104
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:46,535
Cochise drew a knife, slashed through the
side

105
00:07:46,575 --> 00:07:49,075
of the tent and escaped into the hills.

106
00:07:49,115 --> 00:07:54,700
Several members of his family, including
his brother, were left behind as hostages.

107
00:07:56,370 --> 00:07:58,665
What followed spiraled out of control.

108
00:07:58,705 --> 00:08:04,120
Cochise returned under a white flag to ask
for his people back. Talks failed.

109
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,005
Apaches seized hostages of their own.

110
00:08:07,045 --> 00:08:10,361
On February 8, Cochise and hundreds of
warriors

111
00:08:10,401 --> 00:08:13,360
attacked, and later killed their captives.

112
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,014
Days later, on February 19, soldiers
hanged Apache

113
00:08:17,054 --> 00:08:21,000
captives near the pass — Cochise's brother
among them.

114
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:25,435
Historians call it the Bascom Affair.

115
00:08:25,475 --> 00:08:28,990
It helped ignite more than a decade of war
across the region.

116
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:37,913
In July 1862, Cochise and his father-in-
law, the leader

117
00:08:37,953 --> 00:08:42,715
Mangas Coloradas, ambushed a Union column
at Apache Pass.

118
00:08:42,755 --> 00:08:45,241
The warriors held the high ground — until
the

119
00:08:45,281 --> 00:08:47,430
soldiers brought up mountain howitzers.

120
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:51,446
One Apache fighter later put it plainly:
they would have

121
00:08:51,486 --> 00:08:55,000
won, if the soldiers hadn't fired wagons
at them.

122
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:01,636
Mangas Coloradas was later seized during
peace talks and killed in Army

123
00:09:01,676 --> 00:09:06,620
custody, and Cochise became the leading
figure of Chiricahua resistance.

124
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:12,350
Peace finally came in 1872 — and it came
through friendship.

125
00:09:12,390 --> 00:09:16,782
A scout and prospector named Tom Jeffords
rode into Cochise's

126
00:09:16,822 --> 00:09:21,505
mountain stronghold, nearly alone, with
General Oliver O. Howard.

127
00:09:21,545 --> 00:09:23,620
Cochise made them wait for days.

128
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:26,843
Then, after long talks, he agreed to peace
in

129
00:09:26,883 --> 00:09:30,210
exchange for a reservation on his own
homeland.

130
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:34,138
Jeffords became the agent — by the Park
Service's account,

131
00:09:34,178 --> 00:09:37,700
the only white man Cochise ever considered
a friend.

132
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:43,775
Two years later, in June 1874, Cochise
died, probably of cancer.

133
00:09:43,815 --> 00:09:48,911
That night, his warriors painted his body
and carried him into the

134
00:09:48,951 --> 00:09:53,425
mountains, and laid him to rest in a place
only they knew.

135
00:09:53,465 --> 00:09:57,395
Jeffords was one of the very few outsiders
who knew where.

136
00:09:57,435 --> 00:10:01,240
He kept that secret until his own death
forty years later.

137
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,635
The location has never been revealed — and
it shouldn't be.

138
00:10:06,675 --> 00:10:10,200
Some places are meant to stay with the
people they belong to.

139
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:16,975
The peace Cochise made did not outlive him
for long.

140
00:10:17,015 --> 00:10:21,585
Within twelve years, his people were on
those trains heading east.

141
00:10:21,625 --> 00:10:26,590
But the mountains he defended are far
older than any war — and far stranger.

142
00:10:32,370 --> 00:10:38,005
Drive about 120 miles east of Tucson and
the desert does something strange.

143
00:10:38,045 --> 00:10:42,598
Out of grassland and mesquite rises a
range of mountains covered

144
00:10:42,638 --> 00:10:47,190
in forest — and inside them, thousands of
stone columns standing

145
00:10:47,230 --> 00:10:50,890
shoulder to shoulder, like an army frozen
in place.

146
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:57,190
The Chiricahua Apache called this place
the land of the standing-up rocks.

147
00:10:57,230 --> 00:11:01,305
The settlers who came later called it the
Wonderland of Rocks.

148
00:11:01,345 --> 00:11:05,194
Even the name of the mountains is old —
"Chiricahua" is

149
00:11:05,234 --> 00:11:08,770
usually traced to an Opata word for "wild
turkey."

150
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:14,720
Walk into Echo Canyon and the trail
squeezes between walls of stone

151
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:18,490
so close together that hikers call one
stretch "Wall Street."

152
00:11:18,530 --> 00:11:23,767
Climb to the Heart of Rocks and the
pillars crowd around you — some tall

153
00:11:23,807 --> 00:11:29,263
and thin as chimneys, others stacked like
coins, others shaped so much like

154
00:11:29,303 --> 00:11:33,929
animals that early visitors gave them
names: Duck on a Rock. The Totem Pole.

155
00:11:33,969 --> 00:11:35,160
Big Balanced Rock.

156
00:11:36,980 --> 00:11:39,145
But none of it was here by accident.

157
00:11:39,185 --> 00:11:43,400
Its raw material arrived in a violent
geological instant.

158
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:50,225
To understand how it got here, you have to
go back about 27 million years.

159
00:11:50,265 --> 00:11:56,076
In what is now the Chiricahua Mountains, a
series of enormous eruptions tore open the

160
00:11:56,116 --> 00:12:01,031
ground at a place geologists call the
Turkey Creek caldera — a collapsed

161
00:12:01,071 --> 00:12:06,440
volcanic crater roughly twelve miles
across and about five thousand feet deep.

162
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:12,520
The biggest of those eruptions threw out
more than a thousand

163
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:16,740
times the material of the 1980 eruption of
Mount St. Helens.

164
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:20,339
Searing clouds of ash and gas raced across
the

165
00:12:20,379 --> 00:12:23,900
land and buried more than 1,200 square
miles.

166
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:32,710
It was just a barren landscape, gray and
pumice-colored, covered with rocks.

167
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:39,476
When the ash settled, it was so hot that
it welded itself together into solid rock

168
00:12:39,515 --> 00:12:45,190
— a pale volcanic stone called rhyolite,
in places more than 1,600 feet thick.

169
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:48,430
Then time went to work.

170
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:51,970
As the rock cooled, it cracked into
vertical joints.

171
00:12:52,010 --> 00:12:54,270
Water slipped into those cracks.

172
00:12:54,310 --> 00:12:57,062
In winter it froze and expanded, prying
the

173
00:12:57,102 --> 00:12:59,855
rock apart a fraction of an inch at a
time.

174
00:12:59,895 --> 00:13:05,870
For millions of years, rain, ice and wind
carved along those lines — until what was

175
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:11,200
left were pillars, spires and boulders
balanced on impossibly thin necks.

176
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:16,784
Some of these balanced rocks weigh
hundreds of tons and sit on

177
00:13:16,823 --> 00:13:20,510
bases that look like they could tip over
in a strong wind.

178
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:24,600
They've stood like that for longer than
anyone has been here to watch.

179
00:13:26,310 --> 00:13:29,936
And from the top of the park, on the
horizon to the north,

180
00:13:29,976 --> 00:13:33,348
the mountains form a shape that many
visitors see as a

181
00:13:33,388 --> 00:13:36,445
giant face lying on its back, looking at
the sky.

182
00:13:36,485 --> 00:13:39,810
Look for the forehead, the nose and the
chin.

183
00:13:39,850 --> 00:13:41,590
It's called Cochise Head.

184
00:13:43,370 --> 00:13:46,995
But the rocks are only part of what makes
these mountains rare.

185
00:13:47,035 --> 00:13:51,460
Some of what lives here was only caught on
camera about a decade ago.

186
00:13:57,250 --> 00:14:02,575
Chiricahua is what scientists call a sky
island: a mountain range that rises

187
00:14:02,615 --> 00:14:07,375
out of the desert so high that it carries
its own islands of forest.

188
00:14:07,415 --> 00:14:10,155
In a single drive, you climb from desert

189
00:14:10,195 --> 00:14:13,600
grassland through oak woodland into pine
and fir.

190
00:14:15,390 --> 00:14:20,437
Because it sits where the Rocky Mountains,
the Sierra Madre of Mexico and two

191
00:14:20,477 --> 00:14:24,930
great deserts meet, species from very
different worlds overlap here.

192
00:14:24,970 --> 00:14:30,015
Coatis — raccoon relatives from the
tropics — roam the canyons.

193
00:14:30,055 --> 00:14:34,736
Around 200 kinds of birds have been
recorded, including the brilliant

194
00:14:34,776 --> 00:14:38,950
elegant trogon that birders travel across
the country to see.

195
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:44,065
And sometimes, something rarer moves
through the dark.

196
00:14:44,105 --> 00:14:47,732
In 2016, a government trail camera
photographed a

197
00:14:47,772 --> 00:14:50,875
wild jaguar in a range just north of here.

198
00:14:50,915 --> 00:14:55,612
The next year, a conservation group
released video of a jaguar in the

199
00:14:55,652 --> 00:15:00,555
Chiricahua Mountains — a cat that local
students named Sombra, "shadow."

200
00:15:00,595 --> 00:15:04,660
The group says its spot pattern matched
the 2016 animal.

201
00:15:06,420 --> 00:15:09,275
Jaguars once roamed the American
Southwest.

202
00:15:09,315 --> 00:15:13,510
Today, a single wandering cat is enough to
make headlines.

203
00:15:15,150 --> 00:15:19,991
The mountains shelter smaller rarities
too: the Chiricahua leopard frog, a

204
00:15:20,031 --> 00:15:25,202
threatened species that has vanished from
most of the places it once lived, and

205
00:15:25,242 --> 00:15:30,215
pines and squirrels whose closest
relatives live far to the south in Mexico.

206
00:15:30,255 --> 00:15:33,348
For many of them, these forested peaks are
an

207
00:15:33,388 --> 00:15:36,590
island — and the desert all around is the
sea.

208
00:15:38,410 --> 00:15:40,445
These mountains also carry scars.

209
00:15:40,485 --> 00:15:45,872
In 2011, the Horseshoe Two Fire burned
more than 220,000 acres of

210
00:15:45,912 --> 00:15:50,965
the range and touched every acre of the
monument in some way.

211
00:15:51,005 --> 00:15:53,898
The forest is still recovering — a
reminder

212
00:15:53,938 --> 00:15:56,530
that this landscape is still changing.

213
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:06,016
Back in Bonita Canyon, the same canyon
where the Buffalo

214
00:16:06,056 --> 00:16:09,620
Soldiers camped, a very different story
was beginning.

215
00:16:11,470 --> 00:16:15,230
Neil Erickson was a Swedish immigrant who
had fought in the

216
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:19,223
Geronimo campaigns as a cavalry sergeant —
and who would later

217
00:16:19,263 --> 00:16:23,280
become the very first ranger of the
Chiricahua National Forest.

218
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:26,855
Emma Peterson, also from Sweden, had
worked as a maid

219
00:16:26,895 --> 00:16:30,160
for the Army commander at Fort Craig, New
Mexico.

220
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:35,115
They met there, married in 1887, and
claimed a homestead in Bonita Canyon.

221
00:16:35,155 --> 00:16:38,009
Neil later wrote that Emma had found the
place and

222
00:16:38,049 --> 00:16:41,290
thought it was the most gorgeous spot she
had ever seen.

223
00:16:43,110 --> 00:16:47,460
Their daughter Lillian was born in 1888 at
the Fort Bowie hospital.

224
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:52,479
As tourists began discovering the rock
formations,

225
00:16:52,519 --> 00:16:55,495
Lillian and her sister began taking in
guests.

226
00:16:55,535 --> 00:16:57,715
They named it Faraway Ranch.

227
00:16:57,755 --> 00:17:01,517
Lillian and her husband Ed Riggs built
some of the first trails

228
00:17:01,557 --> 00:17:04,715
through the rocks — and lobbied hard to
protect them.

229
00:17:04,755 --> 00:17:10,640
In 1924, President Calvin Coolidge
proclaimed Chiricahua National Monument.

230
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:16,883
A decade later, in the depths of the Great
Depression, a camp of

231
00:17:16,923 --> 00:17:20,735
young men from the Civilian Conservation
Corps arrived.

232
00:17:20,775 --> 00:17:23,524
They built roads, trails, a tunnel, a fire

233
00:17:23,564 --> 00:17:26,645
lookout and the stone overlook at Massai
Point.

234
00:17:26,685 --> 00:17:31,635
At one point, seventeen of them worked
barefoot because their shoes hadn't

235
00:17:31,675 --> 00:17:36,260
arrived — until a telegram to a U.S.
senator finally got them boots.

236
00:17:37,910 --> 00:17:40,860
And through it all, Lillian kept the ranch
running.

237
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:43,880
She had struggled with her hearing since
childhood.

238
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:46,775
In 1942 she lost her sight.

239
00:17:46,815 --> 00:17:50,070
By the end of her life she was blind and
nearly deaf.

240
00:17:51,920 --> 00:17:55,968
She kept running Faraway Ranch anyway —
managing guests,

241
00:17:56,008 --> 00:17:59,545
cattle and staff — for nearly three more
decades.

242
00:17:59,585 --> 00:18:04,076
A magazine writer who stayed with her in
the late 1950s gave her

243
00:18:04,116 --> 00:18:08,080
the nickname that stuck: the Lady Boss of
Faraway Ranch.

244
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:12,225
Her public face was famously cheerful.

245
00:18:12,265 --> 00:18:17,262
Her private diary, preserved by the Park
Service, tells a harder story — of

246
00:18:17,302 --> 00:18:21,600
loneliness, worry and the weight of
holding everything together.

247
00:18:23,330 --> 00:18:28,435
She closed the guest ranch in 1970 and
died in 1977.

248
00:18:28,475 --> 00:18:31,607
Today, Faraway Ranch is part of the park,
and you

249
00:18:31,647 --> 00:18:34,430
can walk through the rooms where she
lived.

250
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:45,755
By the early 1880s, the Apache wars were
not the only violence in these mountains.

251
00:18:45,795 --> 00:18:50,176
Silver had been found across southeastern
Arizona, and boomtowns

252
00:18:50,216 --> 00:18:54,804
appeared almost overnight — Tombstone to
the west, and a rough camp

253
00:18:54,844 --> 00:18:58,850
on the eastern slope of the Chiricahuas
called Galeyville.

254
00:19:00,650 --> 00:19:03,215
Galeyville lasted barely two years.

255
00:19:03,255 --> 00:19:06,535
At its peak, a few hundred people lived
there.

256
00:19:06,575 --> 00:19:11,708
Popular history remembers it as the
hideout of the Cowboys — a loose gang of

257
00:19:11,748 --> 00:19:16,882
rustlers and gunmen led by figures like
Curly Bill Brocius and Johnny Ringo,

258
00:19:16,922 --> 00:19:21,210
the same men locked in a feud with Wyatt
Earp and his brothers.

259
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:26,453
One night in May 1881, a drunk Curly Bill
picked a

260
00:19:26,493 --> 00:19:29,955
fight in Galeyville with a man named Jim
Wallace.

261
00:19:29,995 --> 00:19:32,620
Wallace shot him through the cheek and
neck.

262
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:34,580
Curly Bill survived.

263
00:19:34,620 --> 00:19:36,790
A court called it self-defense.

264
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:41,550
Curly Bill's real ending is its own
mystery.

265
00:19:41,590 --> 00:19:45,730
In March 1882, Wyatt Earp said he killed
him with a shotgun

266
00:19:45,770 --> 00:19:49,485
in a gunfight at a spring in the Whetstone
Mountains.

267
00:19:49,525 --> 00:19:53,658
Tombstone's Nugget offered a thousand
dollars for proof he was

268
00:19:53,697 --> 00:19:57,830
dead; the Epitaph offered two thousand for
proof he was alive.

269
00:19:57,870 --> 00:19:59,855
Nobody ever collected.

270
00:19:59,895 --> 00:20:01,890
His grave has never been found.

271
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:08,025
As for Galeyville, the silver turned out
to be poorer than anyone hoped.

272
00:20:08,065 --> 00:20:11,931
The smelter was hauled away, the post
office

273
00:20:11,971 --> 00:20:15,215
closed in 1882, and the town emptied.

274
00:20:15,255 --> 00:20:17,847
Today nothing is left standing — just

275
00:20:17,887 --> 00:20:21,230
foundations and old mine tailings in the
grass.

276
00:20:24,910 --> 00:20:28,548
Just to the southeast lay Skeleton Canyon,
a smuggling route

277
00:20:28,588 --> 00:20:31,920
to Mexico said to be named for the bones
left along it.

278
00:20:31,960 --> 00:20:38,475
In 1879 and again in 1881, Cowboys
ambushed Mexican travelers there.

279
00:20:38,515 --> 00:20:42,191
Out of those attacks grew a legend: a
treasure of gold and

280
00:20:42,231 --> 00:20:45,330
silver supposedly buried somewhere in the
canyon.

281
00:20:45,370 --> 00:20:48,540
Treasure hunters have searched for more
than a century.

282
00:20:48,580 --> 00:20:53,635
Arizona Highways magazine called it just
another faded and doubtful legend.

283
00:20:53,675 --> 00:20:54,910
People still look.

284
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:58,920
Which brings us back to the man under the
oak.

285
00:21:00,750 --> 00:21:05,410
In July 1882, Johnny Ringo had been
drinking heavily for days.

286
00:21:05,450 --> 00:21:09,835
A deputy met him on the road carrying two
bottles of whiskey.

287
00:21:09,875 --> 00:21:15,740
On the afternoon of July 13, people at a
nearby ranch heard a single shot.

288
00:21:15,780 --> 00:21:18,433
The next day, his body was found in West

289
00:21:18,473 --> 00:21:21,570
Turkey Creek valley, within sight of the
road.

290
00:21:25,950 --> 00:21:27,625
Here is what the record says.

291
00:21:27,665 --> 00:21:29,480
He was sitting against the tree.

292
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:32,840
Revolver in his right hand, rifle nearby.

293
00:21:32,880 --> 00:21:36,545
Boots missing, feet wrapped in strips of
his undershirt.

294
00:21:36,585 --> 00:21:40,060
Two cartridge belts — one of them on
upside down.

295
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:42,824
And part of his scalp gone, in the words
of the

296
00:21:42,864 --> 00:21:45,570
Tombstone Epitaph, "as if cut out by a
knife."

297
00:21:47,490 --> 00:21:50,665
Eleven local men served as the coroner's
jury.

298
00:21:50,705 --> 00:21:53,015
None had forensic training.

299
00:21:53,055 --> 00:21:56,430
They ruled it suicide, and the newspaper
noted that

300
00:21:56,470 --> 00:21:59,550
Ringo had often spoken of taking his own
life.

301
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:03,670
But the strange details never went away.

302
00:22:05,430 --> 00:22:10,076
Wyatt Earp? The theory comes mostly from a
memoir later exposed as

303
00:22:10,116 --> 00:22:13,460
unreliable — and Earp himself publicly
denied it.

304
00:22:15,230 --> 00:22:18,360
Doc Holliday — the version Hollywood
loves?

305
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:21,180
Court records place him in Colorado at the
time.

306
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:24,517
"Buckskin" Frank Leslie?

307
00:22:24,557 --> 00:22:28,829
He was reportedly seen trailing Ringo,
later boasted about the

308
00:22:28,869 --> 00:22:33,420
killing, and one man who accused him was
soon shot dead by Leslie.

309
00:22:33,460 --> 00:22:34,710
But there's no proof.

310
00:22:36,550 --> 00:22:39,120
A gambler known as "Johnny-Behind-the-
Deuce"?

311
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:41,660
A rumor, with a lot of reasons to doubt
it.

312
00:22:43,430 --> 00:22:48,155
One historian's educated guess is that
something spooked Ringo's horse, that

313
00:22:48,195 --> 00:22:52,355
he was caught on foot, and that someone
shot him and took a trophy.

314
00:22:52,395 --> 00:22:55,310
Others still believe the jury got it
right.

315
00:22:57,130 --> 00:23:01,740
Johnny Ringo was buried right where he was
found, at the foot of that tree.

316
00:23:01,780 --> 00:23:04,935
The grave is still there, on private land.

317
00:23:04,975 --> 00:23:09,460
And the case is still open — in the only
court that's left, the court of history.

318
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:20,300
In March 2026, the House passed the
Chiricahua National Park Act.

319
00:23:20,340 --> 00:23:24,275
On September 30 the Senate approved it
unanimously.

320
00:23:24,315 --> 00:23:30,550
On October 20, Chiricahua became the 64th
national park in the United States.

321
00:23:32,110 --> 00:23:42,150
For more than a century… one of the most
biologically

322
00:23:42,190 --> 00:23:52,270
diverse mountain ranges in the entire
Southwest.

323
00:23:53,260 --> 00:23:54,995
The boundaries didn't change.

324
00:23:55,035 --> 00:23:57,710
The name did — and so did the attention.

325
00:23:57,750 --> 00:24:02,315
The law also requires the Park Service to
protect traditional cultural and

326
00:24:02,355 --> 00:24:06,981
religious sites, to consult with tribes,
and to guarantee access for tribal

327
00:24:07,021 --> 00:24:11,275
members — including temporary closures
when a tribe asks for privacy.

328
00:24:11,315 --> 00:24:14,025
The Chiricahua Apache are still here.

329
00:24:14,065 --> 00:24:19,046
Their descendants live today with the Fort
Sill Apache Tribe in Oklahoma and at

330
00:24:19,086 --> 00:24:23,980
Mescalero in New Mexico, and their
connection to these mountains never ended.

331
00:24:25,810 --> 00:24:29,870
It's a quiet place. One campground. No
lodge.

332
00:24:29,910 --> 00:24:32,991
An International Dark Sky Park, where the
stars are as

333
00:24:33,031 --> 00:24:35,920
sharp as they were when Cochise looked up
at them.

334
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:39,080
A volcano built it.

335
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:40,495
Ice carved it.

336
00:24:40,535 --> 00:24:43,544
Warriors defended it, outlaws hid in it, a
blind

337
00:24:43,584 --> 00:24:46,760
woman kept it, and a jaguar has walked
through it.

338
00:24:48,540 --> 00:24:51,229
Welcome to Chiricahua — America's newest

339
00:24:51,269 --> 00:24:54,340
national park, and one of its oldest
stories.

340
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:59,571
If you want more stories like this — the
hidden history

341
00:24:59,611 --> 00:25:03,575
behind America's most remarkable places —
subscribe, and tell

342
00:25:03,615 --> 00:25:07,160
us in the comments which place we should
explore next.

